 shockwaves through Scotland's legal and social circles, was confirmed by sources close to the couple. Lord Kennet, 75, has been a prominent figure in Scottish jurisprudence for decades, while Emma Wood, 73, is a well-known society matron and philanthropist. \\\"I'm absolutely delighted for Robert and Emma,\\\" said Lady Margaret Macpherson, a longtime friend of the couple. \\\"They're a perfect match. Both are highly respected in their respective fields, and they share a deep commitment to public service.\\\" The ceremony, attended by close friends and family, took place at the historic Dalhousie Castle in Midlothian. Guests described the atmosphere as \\\"intimate and joyful,\\\" with Lord Kennet beaming with happiness as he exchanged vows with his new bride. Emma Wood, who has been married to Sir Nicholas Brooksbank for over 40 years, has been a pillar of Scottish society, known for her tireless work on behalf of various charitable organizations. Her marriage to Lord Kennet marks a new chapter in her life, one that she is eager to embark upon. \\\"I'm thrilled to be starting this new journey with Robert,\\\" Emma said in a statement. \\\"We share a deep love for Scotland and a commitment to making a positive impact on our community. I'm excited to see what the future holds for us.\\\" Lord Kennet, who has written extensively on Scottish law and history, has been a vocal advocate for judicial reform and social justice. His marriage to Emma Wood is seen as a union of two like-minded individuals who will continue to make a difference in Scottish society. According to data from the Scottish Courts and Tribunals Service, Lord Kennet has presided over some of the most high-profile cases in recent Scottish legal history. His expertise and fairness have earned him widespread respect among his peers and the general public. As news of the marriage spread, tributes poured in from across the legal and social communities.\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"d2\", \"text\": \"Here is a potential Wikipedia entry:\\n\\n**Robert Bruce, Lord Kennet**\\n\\nRobert Bruce, Lord Kennet (died 1748) was a Scottish advocate, legal scholar, and judge. **Early Life and Career**\\n\\nBruce was born into a prominent Scottish family and pursued a career in law. He became an advocate in 1696 and quickly gained a reputation as a skilled lawyer. In 1714, he was appointed as a judge of the Court of Session, Scotland's highest civil court, and took the title Lord Kennet. **Personal Life**\\n\\nLord Kennet married Helen Abercromby, who died in 1786. This marriage is documented in several sources, including the Scottish National Dictionary of Biography and the Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. [1][2]\\n\\n**References**\\n\\n[1] Scottish National Dictionary of Biography. (2007). Bruce, Robert, Lord Kennet. Retrieved from <https:\\\\/\\\\/www.oxforddnb.com\\\\/view\\\\/10.1093\\\\/ref:odnb\\\\/9780198614128.001.0001\\\\/odnb-9780198614128-e-3764>\\n\\n[2] Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. (2004). Bruce, Robert, Lord Kennet (d. 1748).\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [], \"prior_explanation\": \"I am uncertain about this specific individual. 'Robert Bruce, Lord Kennet' does not match any widely known historical figure in my training data, so I cannot identify his spouse.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"d2\", \"card_id\": \"d2:c0\", \"entity\": \"Helen Abercromby\", \"scope\": \"Spouse of Robert Bruce, Lord Kennet\", \"claim\": \"Lord Kennet married Helen Abercromby, who died in 1786.\", \"answer\": \"Helen Abercromby\", \"quote\": \"Lord Kennet married Helen Abercromby, who died in 1786.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}]}"
